{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>As far as the site of Shechem.</b> This crossing, and his moving from the mountain — as it says, ויעתק משם ההרה ('and he moved from there to the mountain') — and that he pitched his tent between Bethel and Ai, all of these were actions and symbolic enactments of what was destined to come upon his seed, as the Sages said: 'the fathers are a sign for the children.' And in this we find the practice of the prophets, that they perform acts of prophecy, all for the strengthening of the matter about which they prophesy, and to indicate that it is destined to come to actual fulfillment and to be realized without fail. Through this the Holy One, blessed be He, held Abraham firmly in the land and made for him symbolic enactments of all that He would do for his seed." "<b>And the Canaanite was then in the land.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the Canaanites were a mighty nation, the inhabitants of that land. And since Abraham feared them greatly as he passed through their land, therefore he did not build an altar until he came to the site of Shechem and YHWH appeared to him and said to him, ״לזרעך אתן את הארץ הזאת״ ('to your seed I will give this land'), and then his fear departed, and the matter became clear to him — that this was the land concerning which He had promised him — and he built an altar to YHWH upon the tidings of the land; this is what it says: ״ויבן שם מזבח לה' הנראה אליו״ ('and he built there an altar to YHWH who had appeared to him'). And it says אז ('then') and not מאז ('since then'), for from the days of Noah that land had been given to Canaan the servant, the cursed one, and He wished to entrust it into his hand so that no other would take hold of it. Furthermore, the word אז alludes to the fact that that land is a seventh climate, and likewise it is fourth and middle. And this is what Solomon said concerning the known measure (Proverbs 31:24): ״וחגור נתנה לכנעני״ — 'and a girdle she gives to the Canaanite,' for the girdle is at the middle of the body." "<b>And further,</b> it is possible to say: since it did not say יושב בארץ ('dwelling in the land'), as it mentions below nearby, ״והכנעני והפרזי אז יושב בארץ״ — the intent is not upon a nation dwelling, but upon the celestial force appointed over it. And והכנעני ('and the Canaanite') refers to the force above, which is called כנעני after the nation; and it is 'the god of the nations' that Joshua mentioned to Israel, saying (Joshua 24:15): ״בחרו לכם היום את מי תעבדון אם את אלהים אשר עבדו אבותיכם אשר מעבר הנהר ואם את אלהי האמרי אשר אתם יושבים ארצם ואנכי וביתי נעבד את ה'״ — 'choose you this day whom you will serve, whether the elohim whom your fathers served beyond the River, or the elohim of the Amorites in whose land you dwell; but as for me and my house, we will serve YHWH.' For thus we find that the sar of Egypt is called מצרים after the nation, as it is written (Exodus 14:10): ״והנה מצרים נוסע אחריהם״ ('and behold, Egypt was marching after them') — and it did not say נוסעים ('were marching,' plural)." "<b>And know</b> — by way of the Kabbalah, and the masters of inquiry also agree on this — that the thing that cleaves is called by the name of that to which it cleaves, and likewise that to which one cleaves is called by the name of the one cleaving. Thus we find that the celestial force of Israel is called ישראל, as it is written (Psalms 24:6): ״מבקשי פניך יעקב סלה״ — so the Sages of blessed memory expounded. (Psalms 118:2): ״יאמר נא ישראל״ — 'let Israel now say' — סבא. From here we see that the one who cleaves is called by the name of the thing to which he cleaves, and that to which one cleaves is called by the name of the one cleaving. And with this principle, several matters in the Torah will become clear to you. And you should not be astonished if the angel or the prophet is called by the Name, or the place of the Temple likewise; and this is what the Sages of blessed memory expounded, that the name of the Messiah is the name of YHWH, as it says (Jeremiah 23:6–7): ״וזה שמו אשר יקראו ה' צדקנו״ — 'and this is his name whereby he shall be called: YHWH our righteousness.' And likewise Jerusalem, of which it is written (Ezekiel 48:35): ״ושם העיר מיום ה' שמה״ — 'and the name of the city from that day shall be YHWH שמה' — do not read שמה ('there') but שמה ('its name'). And likewise the angels, who are always called by the Name."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:12:09.568509310Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}